        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "> Thanks for suggestion!\r\n> \r\n> I think using autocommand on `ColorScheme` event is the best way for this kind of tweaking. Alternatively, if user wants to tweak only one particular color scheme, then those commands can be done only after corresponding `:colorscheme` command.\r\n> \r\n> This decision is mostly based on the fact, that there are many possible ways which users might want to tweak color scheme. It would not benefit overall maintainability and loading speed of color scheme.\r\n> \r\n> Sorry, but I don't see this as part of 'mini.base16' (or 'mini.hues' for that matter).\r\n\r\nFair enough - My perspective was that enabling or disabling backgrounds provides a different level of flexibility compared to, for instance, directly changing the background color to a specific shade - in short, it doesn't require granular adjustments to individual color values",
